Kubernetes Troubleshooting Cheat Sheet
1. Pod Pending

Problem: Pod is created but not getting scheduled to a node.

Check
------
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get events -n <namespace> --sort-by=.lastTimestamp
Look at the Events section of describe.

Common causes
-------------
Not enough CPU/memory
Node selector doesn't match
Taints/tolerations problem
PVC not available
Affinity/anti-affinity problem


Taints/Tolerations — simple meaning
They are a way to control which Pods are allowed to run on which Nodes.
Think of it like:
Taint = "This node says: Don't schedule Pods here."
Toleration = "This Pod has permission to run on that tainted node."

Tainted node means a Kubernetes node that has a taint applied to it.

Very simply:
Node
  ↓
Taint applied
  ↓
"Don't allow normal Pods here"

For example:  kubectl taint nodes node1 app=database:NoSchedule
Now node1 is a tainted node.

A normal Pod tries to run there:  Pod ❌ → node1
A Pod with the matching toleration:  Pod + toleration ✅ → node1

So remember:
Taint = restriction on Node
Toleration = permission on Pod

If taint problem:
kubectl describe node <node-name>

Either remove the taint:  kubectl taint nodes <node-name> key=value:NoSchedule-
or add the appropriate toleration to the pod.
Verify  kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Taint/Toleration troubleshooting
1. Check why the Pod is Pending

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look at Events. You may see:
0/3 nodes are available:
1 node(s) had untolerated taint
This means the Pod does not have a toleration matching the node's taint

Normal application Deployment
You don't add any toleration:
This Pod can run on:
node1 ✅
node2 ✅
node3 ❌

Because node3 has:  app=database:NoSchedule
and the nginx Pod doesn't tolerate it.





Check the node's taint
kubectl describe node <node-name>
or:
kubectl get nodes
You may find:  Taints: app=database:NoSchedule

Fix it — Option 1: remove the taint
If the node should accept normal Pods:  kubectl taint nodes <node-name> app=database:NoSchedule-
The - at the end means remove the taint.

 Fix it — Option 2: add toleration
If the node is intentionally reserved for database Pods, add a toleration to the Deployment:

spec:
  template:
    spec:
      tolerations:
        - key: "app"
          operator: "Equal"
          value: "database"
          effect: "NoSchedule"
full file
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: database
spec:
  replicas: 1

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: database

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: database

    spec:
      tolerations:
        - key: "app"
          operator: "Equal"
          value: "database"
          effect: "NoSchedule"

      containers:
        - name: database
          image: mysql:8
          ports:
            - containerPort: 3306

For example, the Node has:  app=database:NoSchedule
This says:  🚫 Don't schedule Pods here unless they have the matching toleration.

Then in the Deployment:
tolerations:
  - key: "app"
    operator: "Equal"
    value: "database"
    effect: "NoSchedule"

This says:  ✅ This Pod accepts the app=database:NoSchedule restriction.

So the flow is:
NODE
app=database:NoSchedule
       ↓
   "Keep Pods out"
       ↓
POD
has matching toleration
       ↓
   "I am allowed"
       ↓
     POD ✅
       ↓
     NODE

In one sentence:  Taint blocks the Pod; toleration allows the Pod to pass the block.
-------------
Example
0/3 nodes are available:
2 Insufficient cpu
1 node(s) had taint

Fix
If insufficient CPU:  kubectl describe nodes

add/scale nodes. or
Reduce the pod's resource request:
Yes. “Reducing the request” means telling Kubernetes that your Pod needs less CPU or memory to be scheduled.

For example, originally:

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"

You change it to:

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"

So you are telling Kubernetes:  “My Pod can start with 100m CPU and 128Mi memory.”


Why?
Suppose the Node has only:  Available CPU = 200m
But Pod asks for:  500m
❌ Pod stays Pending.

If you reduce the request to:  100m
✅ The Node has enough capacity, so Kubernetes can schedule the Pod.

Request = minimum resources Kubernetes reserves/considers for scheduling.
Limit = maximum resources the container is allowed to use.

Exactly — 500m in requests does NOT mean the Pod will always use 500m CPU.
It means:  “Kubernetes, please schedule/reserve this Pod assuming it needs 500m CPU.”


1 CPU   = 1000m
0.5 CPU = 500m
0.1 CPU = 100m

limits:
  cpu: "500m"
  memory: "256Mi"

and

limits:
  cpu: "1"
  memory: "256Mi"

