Node selector doesn't match” means:   The Pod is asking Kubernetes to run on a Node with a specific label, but no Node has that label.

normally we do NOT tell nodeSelector in a Deployment.
If you don't specify it, Kubernetes is free to schedule the Pod on any suitable Node.

So Kubernetes does roughly:
Deployment
    ↓
creates Pod
    ↓
Scheduler checks available Nodes
    ↓
Node 1 suitable? → Yes → schedule there
Node 2 suitable? → Yes → maybe there


When do we use nodeSelector?
Only when you specifically want the Pod on certain Nodes.

For example, you have:
Node 1 → normal
Node 2 → SSD
Node 3 → GPU

If nodeSelector is not specified, Kubernetes scheduler can place the Pod on any suitable Node. 
If it is specified, the Pod can only be scheduled on Nodes matching that label.




Add a label to a Node
kubectl label node node1 hardware=gpu
kubectl label node <new-node-name> hardware=gpu
Now:  kubectl get nodes --show-labels

Use that label in your Deployment

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

metadata:
  name: nginx-deployment

spec:
  replicas: 2

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx

    spec:
      nodeSelector:
        hardware: gpu

      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx:latest
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80

What happens here?
First, label a Node:   kubectl label node node3 hardware=gpu

Now Kubernetes sees:
Deployment
    ↓
Pod
    ↓
nodeSelector:
    hardware: gpu
    ↓
Scheduler looks for Node
    ↓
node3
hardware=gpu
    ↓
Pod runs on node3
