Warning FailedScheduling means:  Kubernetes tried to find a suitable node for the Pod, but could not find one.

FailedScheduling can cause the Pod to remain in Pending state.
Example:
Pod
 ↓
Pending
 ↓
Scheduler tries to find a node
 ↓
FailedScheduling
 ↓
Pod cannot be placed on any node

For example:
Warning  FailedScheduling
0/2 nodes are available:
2 Insufficient cpu

This means:

Pod needs CPU
     ↓
Kubernetes checks Node 1 ❌
Kubernetes checks Node 2 ❌
     ↓
No suitable node
     ↓
Pod stays Pending

Another example:

Warning  FailedScheduling
0/2 nodes are available:
2 node(s) had untolerated taint

This means:
Node 1 → Taint ❌ Pod has no toleration
Node 2 → Taint ❌ Pod has no toleration
             ↓
       Pod cannot be scheduled
             ↓
          Pending

this comes under Not enough CPU/memory

Not exactly.
FailedScheduling is a general message, not specifically a CPU/memory problem.

It can happen because of several reasons:
Pod Pending
   ↓
FailedScheduling
   ↓
Why?
   ├── Not enough CPU/memory
   ├── Taint / missing toleration
   ├── NodeSelector doesn't match
   ├── Affinity rules
   └── Other scheduling restrictions



Check with:  kubectl get pods

You might see:
NAME    READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
nginx   0/1     Pending   0          2m

Then:  kubectl describe pod nginx

You may see:
Warning  FailedScheduling
0/2 nodes are available: 2 Insufficient cpu       it is specifically a CPU resource problem.

Meaning: The Pod is Pending because Kubernetes couldn't find a node where it can run.
