2. Pod Anti-Affinity
This is different.

Anti-affinity means:  "Don't put my Pod on the same node as certain other Pods."

Suppose you have:
Node 1
Node 2

And you already have:  Pod A → Node 1
Now you want another copy of the same application.
You can tell Kubernetes:  "Don't put two copies of this application on the same node."
That is Pod Anti-Affinity.

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: myapp

spec:
  replicas: 2

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: myapp

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: myapp

    spec:
      affinity:
        podAntiAffinity:
          requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
            - labelSelector:
                matchExpressions:
                  - key: app
                    operator: In
                    values:
                      - myapp

              topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname

      containers:
        - name: myapp
          image: nginx

podAntiAffinity:

It means:  "Don't place this Pod together with another Pod matching app=myapp."
So Kubernetes tries:
Node 1 → Pod 1 ✅
Node 2 → Pod 2 ✅
This is good because if Node 1 fails, Pod 2 is still on Node 2.


What causes Pending?
Suppose you have only one node:
Node 1
  ↓
Pod 1

You request 2 replicas:  replicas: 2
And anti-affinity says:  "Don't put both Pods on the same node."

Kubernetes tries:
Pod 1 → Node 1 ✅
Pod 2 → Node 1 ❌
There is no Node 2.

Therefore:
Pod 1 → Running
Pod 2 → Pending

You may see:
FailedScheduling
0/1 nodes are available:
1 node(s) didn't match pod anti-affinity rules

if kubernetes has 2 node means automatically do same 2pods 1 in node1 
Kubernetes does NOT automatically guarantee 1 Pod per node.

Suppose you have:
Node 1
Node 2

and your Deployment has:  replicas: 2

Kubernetes may do:
Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 2 → Pod 2

or it may do:
Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 1 → Pod 2
Node 2 → nothing

Both can be valid.

Why?
The scheduler chooses a suitable node based on things like:
CPU/memory availability
node selectors/affinity
taints/tolerations
other scheduling rules

It does not mean:  2 nodes + 2 Pods = automatically 1 Pod on each node.
If you specifically want 1 Pod on each node
You use Pod Anti-Affinity or topology spread constraints.
For example:
Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 2 → Pod 2

And if you have 3 nodes and 3 replicas:
Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 2 → Pod 2
Node 3 → Pod 3


Without anti-affinity
2 Nodes:
Node 1
Node 2

replicas: 2
Kubernetes could choose:

Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 1 → Pod 2
Node 2 → nothing

Or:
Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 2 → Pod 2
Both are possible.

With required Pod anti-affinity
You are telling Kubernetes:  "These two Pods must not be on the same node."

Then:
Node 1 → Pod 1 ✅
Node 2 → Pod 2 ✅

So with 2 nodes + 2 replicas + required anti-affinity, you get 1 Pod on each node.

And if you had only 1 node + 2 replicas + required anti-affinity:
Node 1 → Pod 1 ✅
Node 1 → Pod 2 ❌
Pod 2 stays Pending, because there is no second node.



Yes, Kubernetes will schedule the Pods automatically. But there is an important distinction:

Without anti-affinity
Kubernetes automatically decides where to put the Pods.
2 Nodes
2 Pods
Kubernetes Scheduler
       ↓
chooses suitable nodes

It may do:
Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 2 → Pod 2
But it is not guaranteed.

With anti-affinity
You tell Kubernetes:  "Do not put these Pods on the same node."
Then Kubernetes scheduler automatically follows that rule:

Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 2 → Pod 2

So:  Kubernetes always does the scheduling automatically.
Anti-affinity controls how Kubernetes should distribute the Pods.



 "not guaranteed" means you cannot depend on Kubernetes automatically putting one Pod on each node.
It means Kubernetes decides automatically, but it does not promise one Pod per node.

Example:

You have:
Node 1
Node 2
replicas: 2
Kubernetes scheduler may choose:
Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 2 → Pod 2

But it could also choose:
Node 1 → Pod 1
Node 1 → Pod 2
Node 2 → nothing
So "not guaranteed" means you cannot depend on Kubernetes automatically putting one Pod on each node.


